{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Upon your enemies and upon your haters.</b> 'Your enemies' — this is Ishmael; 'your haters' — this is Edom. And so Micah prophesied concerning them for the future and said: ״תרום ידך על צריך וכל אויביך יכרתו״ — and these are the two nations under whom we are enslaved and among whom we are scattered. Therefore the Rabbis of blessed memory brought a parable about two geese, which is what they said: Rabba bar bar Ḥanna said: Once I was walking in the wilderness and I saw those two geese whose wings were fatty, and a thread of honey was dripping from them. I said to them: Do we have something like you for the world to come? — one that has no wing, and one that has no thigh. When I came before Rabbi Elazar, he said to me: Israel are destined to give the judgment — end of quotation.\n\nAnd the explanation of the matter is that this sage saw in the holy spirit, in the wilderness — which is a place of solitude — the virtue of these two kingdoms: their fatness and their fullness of every good, their bellies being filled with our delicacies. And he asked them: Do we have a portion in the abundance of your goodness in the future? 'One that has no wing' — the first one raised its wings; this is Ishmael, and it was a sign that they are destined to fly and to flee to us and to dwell in our shade, and that they will give us their animals to offer from them a sacrifice, as it is written: ״כל צאן קדר יקבצו לך״, and so forth. And furthermore, a sign that they will convert and hold fast to the commandment of tefillin, which resemble wings — and this was the raising of the wings. 'And one that has no thigh' — this is Edom, who raised up for himself the thigh, and it is a sign for the commandment of circumcision, that they will convert and enter into a covenant with us. And the intention is that since they are destined to join with us in conversion and to become one people, it goes without saying that the dominion and the kingdom will return to us — which is an allusion to their fat and to the honey that is added from it.\n\nAnd [the meaning of] 'Israel are destined to give the judgment' is that they do not repent, and they delay in converting.\n\nAnd the reason he mentioned 'your enemies' concerning Ishmael and 'your haters' concerning Esau is because the enemy is greater than the hater. For the hater, even though he harms him, will act with a measure of mercy; but the enemy is one in whose heart there is eternal enmity — he will have no mercy upon an elder, he will make his yoke very heavy. And this is from the secret of the language and its precision: for 'enemy' (אויב) is from the root of 'woe' (אבוי), and their letters are identical, on account of the fact that the one who falls into his hand cries out 'woe' and 'alas' (אוי ואבוי).\n\nAnd the reason he began with 'your enemies' is so as to attach the pursuit to 'your haters,' who are the descendants of Esau — for the root of our exile, wherever it is, is among the descendants of Edom. And he called them 'your haters' in the manner of what is written: ״ואת עשו שנאתי״. And so they said in the Midrash of Mishlei: ״תחת שנואה כי תבעל״ — this is Esau, as it is said: ״ואת עשו שנאתי״; ״ושפחה כי תירש גבירתה״ — this is Ishmael, who comes from Hagar. And because the descendants of Ishmael are harder against Israel than the descendants of Esau, therefore Scripture called them 'your enemies.' And so they said: 'Under Edom, and not under Ishmael.'\n\nAnd so we find concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, that He said regarding the Rabim: ״לשונאי״; but concerning His enemies it is said: ״ואויבי ה' כיקר כרים כלו כעשן כלו״. This teaches you that the enemies have no survival at all, and therefore He likens them to the fat of sheep, all of which is consumed like smoke.\n\nAnd these two nations Solomon called 'two daughters,' and he mentioned that they are destined for Gehenna, as he said: ״לעלוקה שתי בנות הב הב״, for he likened Gehenna — which is the place of judgment and justice for the souls of the wicked — to a leech, which draws out the blood, which is the soul.\n\nAnd concerning them Isaiah, peace be upon him, said: ״המתקדשים והמטהרים אל הגנות״. 'The consecrating ones' (המתקדשים) — these are the descendants of Edom, whose custom is to wave their fingers hither and thither. 'And the purifying ones' (המטהרים) — these are the descendants of Ishmael, whose custom is to wash their hands and their feet but not their hearts, which is the essential thing. ״אחר אחד בתוך״ — an allusion to the mixture and intermingling of Edom, who consecrate themselves in it [i.e., idolatry]; or one may say ״אחר אחד בתוך״ — on the day of their festival fixed for them: these on the sixth day, before the Sabbath, and these after the Sabbath, and they leave us trapped in between.\n\n״אוכלי בשר החזיר״ — these are the descendants of Edom. ״השקץ והעכבר״ — these are the descendants of Ishmael. ״יחדו יסופו נאם ה'״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The comment opens with the verse lemma but proceeds without an explicit method-header (על דרך…); the Midrashic material is woven throughout. Abbreviations ישמעאל/אדום expanded silently where the full form is restored from context. The leech verse is Proverbs 30:15; the Isaiah passage is Isaiah 65:3–5 (״אחר אחד בתוך״, ״אוכלי בשר החזיר״, etc., are from Isaiah 66:17)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:35.842828149Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}